Subject: State Evolution

 >According to Paul Johnson's interpretation of recent history in his book
 >Modern Times, the reason why the USSR dissolved is because the leader(s)
 >of the CCCP opposed use of the military to crush domestic insurgency, as
 >was done by the Butchers of Beijing in Tiananmen Square just a short while
 >earlier.

I'd have thought that the initial determinants were the degree of popular
support for the insurgents, and whether the military's rank and file would
actually fire at their own people. When the USSR's 'Committee' realised the
strength of Yeltsin's following, and viewed pictures of his facing down the
tanks, it must have had some bearing on their calculations as to the
risk/reward ratio of persevering with their putsch.

To have absolute power you'd want full control of the military, and be able
to suppress opposition support. (Not easy to do this indefinitely when the
ordinary citizen has to queue for hours to buy bread, meat, razor blades,
soap etc whilst a privileged few eat caviar in their dachas. That's socialism
for you!) Now that Yeltsin's honeymoon period has long passed, the balance has
shifted, but probably not enough. I don't think the Russian people will believe
that their current mess is unrelated to the policies that were followed for
most of this century.

 >Somehow, socialism came to mean redistributionism in the USA.  While not
 >absent in practice, redistrbutionism seems to be largely dead in theory
 >in the USA.  Perhaps our contributors from outside the USA can fill us in
 >on the state of affairs there.

 >Tim Starr - Renaissance Now! - Think Universally, Act Selfishly

In the UK, redistributionism seems to be going out of favour, as can be seen
in changes in Labour Party policy. 

In April 1992, Neil Kinnock (Neil who?!) fought the General Election on a
redistributionist platform. He had long been claiming that the Labour Party's
extra spending plans could be financed through 'growth', and from increased
taxes for the rich. (All parties apart from the Greens would claim to be 'for
growth'.) Those earning less than 20,000 UK pounds per annum would certainly
not be hit by higher taxes. However, this stretched credulity too far. Even
Joe Sixpack would not swallow this! So the party admitted that it would have
to increase taxes across the board, and the figure quoted was a 1% increase in
the basic rate of income tax. 

Also the Conservatives claimed that a vote for Labour would mean immediate
increases in costs for homebuyers due to higher interest rates being necessary
to support a plunging pound. (In the event, this was what happened under the
Conservatives, until the foolish policy of an Exchange Rate Mechanism [semi-
fixed exchange rates distorting currencies from their true market rates] had
to be jettisoned. The forex markets proved to be much more powerful than
governments and central banks, and interest rates were allowed to fall.)
   
But the stage was set. Most people believed that they personally would be
worse off under a Labour government, with 1% tax increases just the thin end
of the wedge. However, during the election campaign, it was still fashionable
to pass oneself off as a philanthropist. Opinion pollsters asked whether it
was a good thing to increase taxes to support National Health Service
expenditure, and whether people preferred tax cuts or increased welfare
spending. And the majority claimed to support higher redistributionism.

As the results from the exit polls were flashed out, it became clear that
the pollsters and pundits were hopelessly wrong. The predicted Labour win, or
hung parliament, materialised into a Conservative majority government. When it
came to a secret ballot, people just voted for what they considered to be in
their own best interests, not what they told the pollsters. 

So now the British Labour Party has done a u-turn following three successive
electoral defeats, abandoning its commitment to tax increases. The architect
of the basic-rate tax increase policy? Former Shadow Chancellor John Smith.
The current Leader of the Opposition? John Smith, who has been keeping a
rather low profile.

However, under the Conservatives we are still subjected to the Therapeutic
nanny State. They legislated compulsory seatbelt wearing for the front seats
of cars, and extended it to cover rear-seat passengers. (I sometimes sling the
belt under my arm to simulate its usage whilst driving through the centre of
major cities, to allow for the increased incidence of cop-cars.) They are
talking about raising petrol tax still further, to help the environment and
cut congestion. And they also want to ban cigarette smoking in public places.
Although I personally do not believe in consuming poisons, (since by definition
poisons are harmful to lifeforms) I think the public cost of someone smoking
outdoors would be negligible, and as for smoking in restaurants, on buses etc,
the decision on whether to prohibit it should be taken by the business owner(s).

As for illegal drugs, if people want to kill themselves or wind up looking
like 'the lights are on, but no one's in', who am I or the State to stop them?
It's just natural selection in action. But if burglaries, muggings and murders
are being committed because the price of drugs is propped artificially high by
the State then this is a clear case of a commons cost. 

Controls on weapons? I bought one of the electrical 'stun-guns' a few years
ago; they sounded rather interesting and I was considering manufacturing my 
own version. They might have deterred an assailant in some cases, but were 
rather tame models. At work I'd been zapped through my jacket, and we'd got
one of the trainees on his bare arm. Then controls on the 'guns' were tightened
up, and one day a couple of cops called round and confiscated the device. (I'd
bought it mail-order, and the authorities had obviously carried out an
investigation. When you are not used to dealing with the police, it's not easy
to lie and concoct instant stories: "I sold it to a man in a pub." "What did
he look like? What pub?"......etc. So I didn't attempt to.) I was given a
receipt, but never got any compensation.

And now we have another cross to bear, with the European Commission's attempts
to regulate the sale of vitamins and nutrients. It wouldn't surprise me if
meat-eating was made illegal some day. John Major was supposed to be Margaret
Thatcher's preferred successor, following her ousting, but turned out to be a
damp squib. In fact, they're having to amplify a 'mini-scandal' over his
alleged association with a woman who does the catering at Downing Street
events, to make him look more interesting. Well, it worked for the Liberal
Democrat leader, Paddy Ashdown (also dubbed 'Paddy Pantsdown'). And for Clinton.

However, I think the definition of socialism should be revised little or not
at all. The alternative is to view all governments as practising socialism to
varying degrees. So under the former we can conclude that socialism is
critically wounded, and statism is the only remaining enemy of the people.
Statism -- enslavement of the masses, for their own 'protection'.

