Date:     Sun, 31 Jan 93 8:35:36 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: ECON: Are Medicare Expenditures Too High?

One of Worley's questions about the market for medical charity is whether
it's likely that, if freed, it would equal the amount of current expenditures
on Medicare and Medicaid, the US Federal subsidies for medicine.

Why should it?  Is it possible for this spending to be too high because the
consumers (patients) aren't paying for their treatment except as taxpayers?

US medical spending as a fraction of GNP started outpacing price inflation
when Medicare and Medicaid were passed in 1964.  Since then until the early
1980s, it skyrocketed.  All medical spending, but especially Federal spending
on medicine, is too high right now.  Not only are unnecessary treatments
being done, but those treatments cost too much.

Unshackling consumer forces in the medical market would bring down the costs
of treatments enormously; in Dallas, some standard blood tests cost 5 times
as much at the most expensive hospitals than at the cheapest.  There isn't
enough variation in the quality of the tests to account for this.  The
most expensive baby deliveries in some areas is more than twice as much as
the cheapest ones.

This sort of problem would be fixed by making consumers price-seekers, which
they would have incentive to be if they were spending money that belonged
to them, not the taxpayers.

There would be no need for a free society to have anywhere near the level of
charity medical care spending as we now have Medicare and Medicaid spending,
because we spend too much on medical care.

For more info, see Cato's two new books: "Patient Power," and "What Has
Government Done to Our Health Care?"

