Subject: ECON: Are Medicare Expenditures Too High?

I suggest that, in a free market, health care expenditures will and
should go _up_.  Not out of lack of efficiency; if insurers were allowed
to "discriminate", consumers were freed from FDA bans and medical
licensure monopolies, etc. the gains in efficiency would be large.  No, 
expenditures will go up because health is the last insatiable need.
All other needs (food, housing, etc.) are satiable, to the point where 
excess (large house, French restaraunt, etc.) is a luxury.  Spending
on food has already decline as a % of GDP; I predict spending on housing
etc. will go the same way.  Unless we consider pushing back death 
indefinitely to be a luxury (and few volunteere to get sick or die), 
health is the only insatiable need our economy.  Thus, in a free market 
I predict health care expenditures will grow indefinetely as a share 
of GDP, perhaps even approaching 100%!

