Date: Sun, 31 Jan 93 19:09 EST
From: kqb@whscad1.att.com
Subject: recovering encrypted records

Reliable encryption methods require reliable security for
the encryption keys.  Storing the key in only one, secret,
secure place helps restrict access to just those who should
have it, but the lack of redundancy also makes it easy to lose,
and thus lose everything encrypted with that key.  Aren't there
some schemes where any M out of N "keys" are sufficient to generate
the real key?  Would that make sense for PGP secret key rings,
if you are not confident of the physical security of any one copy
(in case the storm troopers come raid your residence)?
Maybe something like that would have helped in the following
situation, where the encryption key was stored in a leaky vault
(the person's mind).  But maybe, in this case, there is a work-around,
since the encryption was not with a system as powerful as PGP.

-----

Somebody on a local BBS encrypted his financial records with
pkzip a few years ago and now has forgotten his encryption key.
Is there any known way to retrieve his records (besides somehow
remembering his encryption key)?

What cryptanalytic attacks might be fruitful?  Since they were
financial records, they should have a lot of numbers in them,
unlike normal English text.  And since he has the pkzip program,
it should be easy to construct a lot of clear text / encrypted text
pairs to see how the encryption works.  (Also, a real bit-head would
just look at the binary code for pkzip, I suppose.)  What else would
be useful?  Thanks.
					Kevin Q. Brown
